namespace molpro{
namespace linalg{
/*!
@page TopicIterativeSolver Iterative solvers

There are four types of systems that iterative-solver can solve:
   - Linear eigensystem
   - Linear equations
   - Non-linear equations
   - Optimization of a scalar function

The interface for different solvers is declared in ``molpro/linalg/itsolv/IterativeSolver.h``.

Linear eigensystem solver is implemented in ``molpro/linalg/itsolv/LinearEigensystemDavidson.h``. It also solves
generalised eigenproblems A v = lambda M v with a Hermitian, positive definite metric M, whose action on the expansion
vectors is supplied along with the action of A (Problem::provides_metric(), Problem::metric_action(),
LinearEigensystem::add_vector()). The solutions are then normalised in the metric, and their residuals are
A v - lambda M v. The errors are the norms of the residuals of the solutions scaled to unit length, |A v - lambda M v| /
|v|, which do not depend on the scale of M, and which are the same as without a metric if M is the identity, so that the
convergence threshold has the same meaning with and without a metric. The eigenvalues, and so the value errors, scale
inversely with M.

Linear eigensystem and linear equations use a novel P space and D space algorithm described in the paper (WIP).
The different kinds of spaces used by the solvers are described in @ref TopicSpaces.

There is significant overlap between different solvers and all common functionalities are declared in IterativeSolver
base class and implemented in IterativeSolverTemplate. This makes it easy to extend the library with new solvers.
See LinearEigensystemDavidson for an example of the structure.

*/
}
}